{{ 'section-gallery.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'component-buy-box.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<section class="sec sec--tight">
  <div class="wrap pdp-grid">

    <div class="gallery" data-gallery>
      <div class="gallery-main" data-gallery-main>
        {%- for image in product.images -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The slide image is a button, not a plain img: tapping it opens the
            full-screen viewer. The 2048px source is only referenced here as a
            data attribute — nothing fetches it until the viewer opens, so the
            PDP still ships the same bytes it did before.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="gallery-slide" data-gallery-slide>
            <button type="button" class="gallery-zoom"
                    data-zoom-open
                    aria-haspopup="dialog"
                    aria-label="Zoom image {{ forloop.index }} of {{ product.images.size }}"
                    data-zoom-src="{{ image | image_url: width: 2048 }}"
                    data-zoom-alt="{{ image.alt | default: product.title | escape }}">
              {%- if forloop.first -%}
                {% render 'responsive-image', img: image, alt: image.alt | default: product.title, widths: '600,900,1200', sizes: '(min-width:900px) 55vw, 100vw', loading: 'eager', priority: true %}
              {%- else -%}
                {% render 'responsive-image', img: image, alt: image.alt | default: product.title, widths: '600,900,1200', sizes: '(min-width:900px) 55vw, 100vw', loading: 'lazy' %}
              {%- endif -%}
              <span class="gallery-zoom-hint" aria-hidden="true">{% render 'icon', name: 'search', size: 16 %}</span>
            </button>
          </div>
        {%- else -%}
          <div class="gallery-slide"><div class="img-missing"><span>Product photography pending — approved library not yet uploaded to this product</span></div></div>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>

      {%- if product.images.size > 1 -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Thumbs above 900px, dots below — the two never share a screen, so
          they are one control at two sizes rather than two controls saying
          the same thing.

          role="tablist" was wrong here: the buttons were never role="tab" and
          the slides are a scroll-snap strip, not tabpanels, so the promise the
          role makes to a screen reader was one this markup could not keep. A
          named group of buttons describes what this actually is, and
          aria-current marks which image is showing.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="gallery-thumbs" role="group" aria-label="Product images">
          {%- for image in product.images -%}
            <button type="button" class="gallery-thumb {% if forloop.first %}is-active{% endif %}"
                    data-gallery-thumb
                    {% if forloop.first %}aria-current="true"{% endif %}
                    aria-label="Show image {{ forloop.index }} of {{ product.images.size }}">
              {% render 'responsive-image', img: image, alt: '', widths: '150', sizes: '76px' %}
            </button>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          These were 6px spans inside an aria-hidden wrapper that theme.js
          nonetheless bound a click handler to: a control no keyboard could
          reach, no screen reader could see, and no thumb could reliably hit —
          on mobile, where they are the only position control there is. Real
          buttons now, each carrying the 44px target the rest of the theme
          holds to, with the 6px dot kept as the visual.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="gallery-dots" role="group" aria-label="Product images">
          {%- for image in product.images -%}
            <button type="button" class="gallery-dot {% if forloop.first %}is-active{% endif %}"
                    data-gallery-dot
                    {% if forloop.first %}aria-current="true"{% endif %}
                    aria-label="Show image {{ forloop.index }} of {{ product.images.size }}"></button>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      data-buybar-anchor: the sticky bar appears the moment this scrolls out of
      view, so the two Add to Cart controls are never on screen at once.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div id="buybox" data-buybar-anchor>
      {% render 'buy-box', product: product, compact: false %}
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

{%- comment -%}
  In-page nav for what is a very long page: six storytelling sections stack
  below the buy box, and until now the only way to know that was to scroll
  through them. Worst on a phone, where that scroll is longest.

  Deliberately not a third sticky bar — the header is already pinned and the
  buy bar already sits at the bottom of the viewport on mobile. This sits in
  the flow at the seam between buying and reading, which is the moment the
  question "how much more is there?" actually gets asked.

  Empty in the markup and filled by theme.js from whichever sections below
  carry data-jump. That way it reflects the sections a merchant actually
  placed, in their real order, and a merchant who clears a section's label
  drops it from the nav. With JS off, or with nothing opted in, it stays empty
  and collapses to nothing.
{%- endcomment -%}
<nav class="pdp-jump" data-pdp-jump aria-label="On this page" hidden></nav>

{%- comment -%}
  Full-screen image viewer. One <img> that gets its source swapped, rather than
  a copy of every slide, so opening the viewer costs exactly one image.
  Rendered outside <section> so nothing in the PDP grid can become a containing
  block for the fixed positioning. Inert (hidden) until theme.js opens it, and
  entirely absent when the product has no photography.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if product.images.size > 0 -%}
  <div class="zoom" data-zoom hidden role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="zoom-title">
    <h2 id="zoom-title" class="visually-hidden">Product image viewer</h2>

    <div class="zoom-bar">
      <p class="zoom-count" data-zoom-count aria-live="polite"></p>
      <button type="button" class="zoom-btn" data-zoom-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Zoom in">
        <span class="zoom-icon-in">{% render 'icon', name: 'plus' %}</span>
        <span class="zoom-icon-out">{% render 'icon', name: 'minus' %}</span>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="zoom-btn" data-zoom-close aria-label="Close image viewer">
        {% render 'icon', name: 'close' %}
      </button>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Panning is native overflow scrolling, so the stage is a real scroll
      container: tabindex makes it reachable and arrow-key scrollable for
      keyboard users once the image is magnified.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="zoom-stage" data-zoom-stage tabindex="0" role="group"
         aria-label="Product image. When zoomed in, drag or use the arrow keys to pan.">
      <img class="zoom-img" data-zoom-img alt="" draggable="false">
    </div>

    {%- if product.images.size > 1 -%}
      <button type="button" class="zoom-nav zoom-prev" data-zoom-prev aria-label="Previous image">
        {% render 'icon', name: 'chevron', size: 24 %}
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="zoom-nav zoom-next" data-zoom-next aria-label="Next image">
        {% render 'icon', name: 'chevron', size: 24 %}
      </button>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Product",
  "settings": []
}
{% endschema %}
